{"archetypes":[{"name":"line-ref-fidelity","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"Plan cites 15+ specific line numbers across design-log-publish.sh, SECURITY.md, lib-quiet.sh, breadcrumb-monitor.sh, ship-pr.sh, and step-7a.sh; none are verified against current file state, so stale references mean fixes land at wrong locations.","prompt_body":"Verify every explicit line-number reference in the plan against the actual current file content at the stated path. For each cited range — design-log-publish.sh:293-295, :320-342, :348-373, :402-405, :408-431, :493; SECURITY.md:~28, :139, :186, :248; breadcrumb-monitor.sh:149; lib-quiet.sh:96-100, :105-122, :172; ship-pr.sh:719-723, :3042; step-7a.sh:368-380; dispatch-code-voters.sh:171; dispatch-plan-voters.sh:142 — confirm that the code at that location matches the construct the plan describes before any fix is applied. Flag any stale reference where the described construct no longer appears at the stated line or the surrounding context contradicts the plan's description, because a stale reference means the implementation edits the wrong location and leaves the stated defect unfixed. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"drop-on-fail-semantics","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"Item 1B introduces a drop-on-fail redaction stage in breadcrumb-monitor.sh where a failed redact-tmpdir-paths.sh silently suppresses operator-visible lines; the plan does not verify FD routing for the WARN diagnostic or confirm the committed-log copy is on a separate code path.","prompt_body":"Trace the exact FD routing for both the new WARN redact-tmpdir-drop-line diagnostic and the surviving redacted breadcrumb line in breadcrumb-monitor.sh to verify the WARN reaches the operator's terminal during a live /implement run rather than being swallowed by a background redirect. Check whether the committed larch-logs breadcrumbs/ copy is written through a genuinely separate code path unaffected by the new redact-tmpdir-paths.sh pipeline stage, or whether both outputs share the same invocation. Determine whether a systematic failure of redact-tmpdir-paths.sh — e.g. missing binary, wrong PATH — would suppress all operator-visible breadcrumbs with no error beyond the single WARN, and assess whether the plan's drop-on-fail semantic is consistent with the fail-closed posture the plan applies elsewhere in the same pipeline. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"linter-coverage-completeness","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"Plan extends lint-foreground-markers.sh to enforce a 4-variable broadened-unset rule for nested Family-B calls but does not enumerate all existing call sites across the tree that must comply, and does not verify the 5-line look-back window accommodates the expanded unset block.","prompt_body":"For each Family-B nested call site the broadened rule targets — dispatch-with-waterfall.sh, review-and-fix.sh, ci-wait.sh — enumerate every invocation in scripts/ and skills/ and verify each will have the 4-variable unset block or an explicit lint-foreground-markers exemption after the plan's edits; call out any call site the plan omits. Check that the planned test subcases (literal, variable, default-expansion) in test-lint-foreground-markers.sh cover each of the three new variable names individually rather than only as a block, so a regression dropping one name is caught. Verify that the linter's 5-line look-back window is wide enough to contain a 4-variable unset block plus any interleaved comment lines at every real call site the plan modifies. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
